---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import UuidGenerator from '../../components/UuidGenerator.jsx';
---

<ToolLayout
  title="UUID Generator"
  description="Generate random UUID v4 identifiers instantly — one or in bulk, click to copy, no signup."
>
  <UuidGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>What is a UUID</h2>
  <p>
    A UUID (Universally Unique Identifier) is a 128-bit value used to
    uniquely identify records — database rows, API resources, session
    tokens — without needing a central authority to guarantee uniqueness.
    UUID v4 is generated using random numbers, making collisions
    astronomically unlikely.
  </p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Set how many UUIDs you need.</li>
    <li>Click Generate.</li>
    <li>Click any UUID to copy it, or use "Copy all" for the full list.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>UUID Generator FAQ</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Are these cryptographically secure?</strong> They use your
    browser's <code>Math.random()</code>, which is fine for test data, keys,
    or non-security-critical identifiers. For security-sensitive tokens (like
    session secrets), use a cryptographically secure random source in your
    backend instead.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Can two generated UUIDs ever collide?</strong> Theoretically
    yes, but the probability is astronomically small — UUID v4 has enough
    randomness that collisions are not a practical concern even at very
    large scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Should I use UUIDs as database primary keys?</strong> They work
    well, though they take more storage than integers and can hurt index
    locality on very large tables — see the SQL Primary Key Generator for a
    comparison with auto-increment and ULID alternatives.
  </p>
</ToolLayout>
